is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
A thorough end-to-end investigation of the simple_bgp topology found no fault at any layer. Phase 1 confirmed ICMP reachability in both directions (pc1↔pc2) with 0% packet loss. Phase A infrastructure sweep found no ACL drop rules, no addressing anomalies, no static ARP poisoning, no L2 identity conflicts, and all interfaces UP on all four devices. Phase B routing sweep confirmed that both FRR BGP daemons (router1/AS1, router2/AS2) are Established, the correct prefixes (195.11.14.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24) are advertised and installed in both routing tables via the correct next-hops, and no TC shaping is applied anywhere. Manual inspection of all iptables chains on every device found all policies ACCEPT with no rules. BGP neighbor detail and FRR debug logs confirm the only "anomaly" — each router re-advertising the remote prefix back to its eBGP peer — is standard eBGP behavior correctly resolved by AS-PATH loop detection (both DENY log entries present and expected). Phase D pressure sweep found no resource contention. Since lab startup, the BGP session has exchanged only keepalives with no route flaps, no withdrawals, and no errors. The user's report of asymmetric "replies getting lost" is not supported by any mechanism, device state, traffic counter, or historical trace in the current topology. Root cause: no fault is present; the network is operating correctly in all respects. Fix: no corrective action is needed; advise the reporter to re-test and verify the original symptom was not a transient client-side or application-layer issue external to this network.
=== END ===